\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{The larger proposals as open tests}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{The [compatibility test](fdip-009Y) fixes one finite spinor
datum. The same 5 criteria now record the larger proposals
left open by [the status of that datum](fdip-00A1). Each
proposal states the claim under test, the algebraic datum
already in hand, the map that is missing, and whether any
finite witness is available. An open mark records that absence.
It is not a theorem that no later construction exists, and it
is not a physical identification.}

\p{The 4 Lie-group names are those already listed as
unconstructed in \ref{fdip-00A1}. Their source claims are
not the same. Three names do not occur in the cited Wilson
paper. #{SL(4,\mathbb R)} does, in a later section, and the
Hessian route is the claim of Section 2.2. Both remarks
below quote the claim that was rechecked and say what is
missing. A correspondence still has to specify an action and
a representation, an algebra map, any preserved structure or
form, a unit-group choice where applicable, and the intended
physical meaning, as required by
[why begin with 2T?](fgap-0018). Resemblance is not a model,
and neither remark below adopts a group.}

\p{The two remarks below keep these proposals open. Neither
remark adopts a group as the symmetry of the lattice theory,
and neither draws a no-go conclusion from the missing map.}

\transclude{fdip-00A3}
\transclude{fdip-00A4}
